18. FIRESTORE SECURITY RULES
============================

Generate a complete, carefully reviewed firestore.rules file.

Do not use permissive placeholder rules.

Never use:
allow read, write: if true;

Never deploy open development rules as production rules.

Default-deny all paths not explicitly authorized.

Use Firebase Authentication and role-based authorization.

Create helper functions for:

* signedIn()
* isAdmin()
* isParent()
* parent-child relationship verification
* required-field validation
* allowed-field validation
* type validation
* safe document updates

ADMIN AUTHORIZATION:
Read the user's role from a protected users/{uid} document or another secure role source.

Require the authenticated user to be active and to have the admin role.

Prevent ordinary users from assigning or changing their own role.

Prevent public users from creating an admin account.

PARENT AUTHORIZATION:
Require the authenticated user to be active and have the parent role.

Require an explicitly authorized parentStudentLinks relationship for access to each child.

Do not authorize access merely because a client supplies a student ID.

COLLECTION-SPECIFIC REQUIREMENTS:

users:

* Users may read their own permitted profile.
* Admins may read permitted user records.
* Prevent client-side role escalation.
* Restrict parent profile updates to an explicit allowlist of safe fields.
* Do not allow a parent to change their UID, role, or active status.
* Prevent unauthorized profile deletion.

students:

* Admins may create and update student records through validated operations.
* Parents may read only individually authorized linked students.
* Parents cannot list every student.
* Parents cannot create, edit, or delete student records.
* Permanent deletion must use the controlled trusted workflow.

parentStudentLinks:

* Only admins may create, update, authorize, or delete relationships.
* A parent may read only their own relationship records.
* Verify the parent UID and student ID against the deterministic document ID.
* Do not permit self-linking.
* Verify referenced records where the rules and available access allow it.

batches:

* Permit authorized reads of necessary schedule information.
* Restrict all writes to admins.
* Validate required fields and field types.
* Prevent unauthorized deletion.

classSessions:

* Permit authorized reads of necessary schedule information.
* Restrict writes to admins.
* Validate batch IDs, timestamps, status, and grace period.
* Prevent unauthorized deletion.

attendance:

* Admins may read attendance records.
* Parents may read attendance only for authorized children.
* Only admins may create or update records through permitted transitions.
* Enforce the deterministic student-session document ID.
* Validate the referenced student, session, and matching batch.
* Do not allow duplicate logical attendance records.
* Enforce valid attendance states and timestamp requirements.
* A PRESENT record requires an arrival timestamp.
* An ABSENT record must not have an arrival or departure timestamp.
* LEFT must require a previous PRESENT state and preserve the original arrival timestamp.
* Departure must not be earlier than arrival.
* Restrict updates to explicitly allowed fields.
* Do not allow parents to modify attendance.
* Do not allow direct client deletion.

feeRecords:

* Admins may create fee records after validating the student and amounts.
* Parents may read only fee records belonging to their linked children.
* Parents cannot create, modify, or delete fees.
* Do not permit arbitrary changes to established fee obligations.
* Define an audited correction workflow for permitted fee adjustments.

payments:

* Admins may create valid payment records.
* Parents may read only payment records belonging to their linked children.
* Parents cannot create, update, delete, reverse, or alter payments.
* Require a positive integer amount in paise.
* Validate supported methods: CASH and UPI.
* Validate the associated student and fee record.
* Require the fee record to belong to the same student.
* Restrict the recordedBy field to the authorized actor.
* Prevent ordinary clients from altering confirmed payments.
* Implement corrections and reversals through a controlled, auditable workflow.
* Do not allow direct client deletion.

announcements:

* Only admins may create and manage announcements.
* Parents may read only active announcements intended for their authorized audience.
* Ensure that batch-targeted announcements cannot be read by unauthorized parents.
* Do not assume that hiding a batch announcement in the UI provides security.

notificationHistory:

* Admins may create notifications through validated operations.
* A parent may read only their own notification records.
* A parent may update only the permitted read-state fields on their own notifications.
* Prevent a parent from changing the recipient UID or notification content.

auditLogs:

* Admins may read authorized audit logs.
* Do not permit ordinary client-side creation, modification, or deletion of sensitive audit entries.
* Generate security-sensitive audit records through a trusted process.

studentExitJobs:

* Only authorized admins may read job information.
* Do not allow arbitrary client-side job-status changes.
* Use a trusted workflow for report verification and deletion execution.

exitReports:

* Restrict report metadata access to authorized admins.
* Do not allow parents to read another student's report.
* Do not expose private report contents through public URLs.
* Restrict privileged report creation and updates to a trusted workflow.

DEFAULT:

* Deny every other operation unless it is explicitly required and authorized.

RULE QUALITY REQUIREMENTS:

* Use request.auth.uid for identity.
* Validate field types and allowed fields.
* Protect immutable identifiers.
* Protect createdAt and other immutable fields.
* Prevent users from bypassing authorization through update operations.
* Validate related-document ownership where necessary.
* Avoid relying on the UI for authorization.
* Do not create circular or unnecessarily expensive rule lookups.
* Consider Firestore rule document-access call limits.
* Ensure queries are compatible with the security rules.
* Document any workflow that cannot be securely implemented using client-side rules alone.

Do not claim the rules are secure merely because the file was generated.

