19. TRUSTED BACKEND AND PRIVILEGED OPERATIONS
=============================================

Identify which operations require trusted server-side execution.

Examples:

* Secure initial administrator provisioning.
* Sensitive payment corrections and reversals.
* Permanent student deletion.
* Multi-collection deletion jobs.
* Protected report storage and verification.
* Sensitive audit-log creation.
* Other privileged operations that cannot be safely implemented solely through client-side rules.

Use a trusted backend only when necessary.

Never place Firebase Admin SDK credentials in the browser.

If a backend is needed, create a clearly separated, documented interface for it.

Prefer least-privilege authorization and idempotent operations.

Do not silently introduce paid infrastructure.

If a required backend depends on Cloud Functions, Cloud Run, Cloud Storage, or another service that requires a billing-enabled project or may incur charges, clearly identify the requirement before enabling it.

Do not pretend that a frontend-only implementation provides secure privileged deletion.

If a trusted backend is not configured, keep the dangerous operation disabled and explain what must be configured.

