21. PERFORMANCE AND FIREBASE SPARK COST CONTROL
===============================================

The initial goal is to use Firebase Spark wherever possible and avoid unnecessary operational costs.

Design for approximately 100 active students, while recognizing that students may remain enrolled for years.

Requirements:

* Paginate large lists.
* Avoid fetching every student's full history on every dashboard load.
* Fetch only the documents required for each screen.
* Avoid redundant listeners and repeated queries.
* Unsubscribe from listeners when components unmount.
* Avoid unnecessary real-time listeners.
* Use batched operations where appropriate.
* Use transactions when an operation requires consistent reads and writes.
* Do not assume that a batch makes an operation atomic across every related business process.
* Avoid automatic high-frequency polling.
* Provide clear loading and retry states.
* Use indexes appropriate to the queries.
* Avoid scheduled deletion jobs.
* Do not automatically delete attendance after 60 days.
* Do not silently enable a paid plan.
* Do not silently enable billing-dependent services.

Explain that free-tier limits are shared usage limits, not a guarantee that every feature is free.

Before enabling any service that requires billing, explain:

1. Why it is needed.
2. Whether a free alternative exists.
3. The expected operational implications.
4. Which manual configuration is required.

Use the current official Firebase pricing information when assessing service costs. Do not hard-code outdated pricing assumptions into the application.

